# Line endings, pinned so colai ships the same on every machine.
#
# The launcher `bin/colai-toolbar` is a POSIX `#!/bin/sh` script, and the
# `.sha256` files it reads are plain text it compares byte-for-byte. With
# Git's default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows, a fresh clone would check
# those out with CRLF — which breaks the shebang on Linux/macOS (`/bin/sh^M:
# bad interpreter`) and appends a stray `\r` to the hash the launcher reads,
# failing the integrity check. So every text file is normalised to LF on
# checkout, on every platform; Node, Cargo and the editors all handle LF fine
# on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one exception: a Windows batch file is dispatched by cmd.exe, which
# expects CRLF. `bin/colai-toolbar.cmd` is the shim that lets `colai-toolbar`
# resolve from cmd/PowerShell (an extensionless file is not on PATHEXT), so it
# has to check out with CRLF even though everything else stays LF.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Binary artifacts: never normalise, never try to diff them.
*.gz    binary
*.exe   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
